  "authorshipStatus": "traditional",
  "author": "bhadrabahu",
  "attributionNote": "Traditionally ascribed to Bhadrabāhu, the fourth of the Śvetāmbara patriarchs. The attribution is traditional rather than documentary, and the work as received is a compilation.",
  "description": "The Śvetāmbara handbook of the lives of the Jinas and the rule of the rainy-season retreat, in twelve chapters: five lectures on the life of Mahāvīra from his descent into the womb to his liberation, the lives of Pārśva, Ariṣṭanemi and Ṛṣabha, the epochs of the intermediate Tīrthaṅkaras, the list of the patriarchs, and the rules for the mendicants' annual confession. It is the text most often copied and illuminated in western India, and the reading of the life of Mahāvīra from it is the centre of the Paryuṣaṇa festival.",
  "historicalContext": "The Kalpa Sūtra belongs to the Śvetāmbara liturgical canon rather than to the twelve Aṅgas, and its text grew over centuries: the lives draw on older hagiography, and the list of patriarchs was continued as the community's history lengthened. Its public reading at Paryuṣaṇa made it the most widely known of all Jain books.",
  "availabilityNote": "Streamed from the Internet Sacred Text Archive view by view at read time. Jacobi's 1884 translation is public domain; the Prakrit text is not included.",
